<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n67443" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">μηλο-φόρος</span></b> , 
			Dor.
		
		<b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">μᾱλ-</span></b> , <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ον</span>, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>bearing
			apples</i>, 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">καρπός</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					E.
					<u>HF</u>
					396
				</span></i>
			 (lyr.); epith. of Demeter at Megara (said to be from <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">μῆλον</span> A), <i><span class="en">
				Paus.
				1.44.3
			</span></i>; and so <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Μαλοφόρος</span> alone at
			Selinus, <i><span class="en">
				<u>IG</u>
				14.268
			</span></i>
			<span class="entity">(v B.C.)</span>; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">οἱ μ.</span> the
			king of Persia's body-guard, <i>because they had gold</i> or
				<i>silver apples</i> at the butt-end of their spears, <i><span class="en">
				Hsch.
			</span></i></div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
